first we go to Peter Navarro Peter  welcome great to have you with us  tonight Martha I know you're all working  very hard there to get everybody on  track and of course one of the things  that leaves people so unnerved is the  impact that it has on their daily life  with regard to work and their job and  being able to go to work and all of that  tell us a little bit about what was put  in place today to alleviate some of that  anxiety well Martha my job here at the  White House is to be a soldier out  making sure the supply chain works so  that we have everything we need to treat  our people so one of the things I did  yesterday morning I got a call from a  company called Pernod Ricard it makes  alcohol it makes  alcoholic beverages they had volunteered  to make a hand sanitizer using their  alcohol they had some obstacles so I  made three phone calls one to Treasury  one day the FDA one to HHS to clear  their obstacles out of the way in Trump  time quickly and today we got a  commitment they're going to start  producing on Friday and over time we're  going to have 4,000 gallons a week and  they're going to be able to send it to  the government at no cost that's the  kind of beauty we're seeing as we have  the full force of government and the  full force of business join hands to  help the American people absolutely by  the way these facilities are going to be  in America they're in West Virginia  Arkansas Texas and Kentucky so I think  one of the things that this is making a  lot of us think about is that supply  chain and one of the areas that is very  concerning is the amount of our medicine  and pharmaceuticals that we have handed  over to the Chinese to to to produce and  I know this is something that's very  important to you as well how quickly can  we ramp up that production here at home  what does that look like well for  example I'll give you another example  Honeywell offered on Friday they called  my office they say look we want to start  with producing within 30 days the  facemasks we can open up a plant in  smithfield rhode island all we need to  do is have you help us work with HHS to  get the ball rolling so on friday i said  okay get your contract and they got it  in by that night I called HHS over the  weekend then said yeah we're gonna flip  that within a couple of days and already  we have Honeywell moving towards that so  what it looks like is having the  appropriate incentives for our domestic  industry to produce these things like  face masks and hand sanitizer and all  the things we need and and what we need  long-term Martha this is this is the  problem we face the government does not  buy American enough the government the  VA HHS DoD tremendously large consumers  of medicines medical supplies and  medical equipment and so what we're  working with the President as we speak  to get an executive order to the finish  line which would provide long-term  incentives for our companies to produce  here and it's not this is not about  China because if you look at the  dispersion of that chain it's across the  world Europe for example Ireland  surprisingly enough Germany Switzerland  we're heavily dependent on their India  as well so what we've got to do is  through the three three things Martha  we're going to have by American we're  going to deregulate so we can get things  here done faster and most importantly  we're going to innovate to keep prices  down there's a process called advanced  manufacturing that we're working in  lightening speed to get switches flipped  on there so that's that's the big  picture we cannot keep having these  public health crises and go back to  sleep like the previous administrations  have this is going to be different this  time and we're going to get through this  and and we're going to be in a better  place over time because we're going to  produce things here in America yeah well  I think you know those are all things  that that had in the right direction  with it what about you know the current  concerns over enough beds enough  hospital facilities the question about  whether or not the Army Corps of  Engineers should be building some  temporary hospitals and medical  facilities we heard Governor Cuomo today  saying you know in some cases you could  move some people out of our hospitals  where the heavier equipment is and if  they're in in a in a healthier situation  not dealing with kovat 19 maybe some of  them could be moved into these temporary  facilities what's going on where's the  progress on on that what can you tell us  yeah Martha it's not really my Wain but  what I can tell you is that there's  action quick action within the Pentagon  working with the White House on those  kind of things what I can tell you  though is on Saturday I got another call  where we had to get some swabs for  testing over from Europe here and we got  the Pentagon basically to send a mil Air  flight over to where we needed FedEx  volunteered some some of its own  delivery planes that plane landed  military plan landed in Memphis FedEx  picked them up and we were able to  disperse 850,000 swabs across this  country so that's good Pentagon's  engaged and we're engaged last question  for you is you know sort of the big  picture that I open the show at tonight  in terms of that national anxiety what's  the mood at the White House and on how  to approach that because one of the  things that's going to happen with all  those additional swabs and all the  additional testing is something that  we've all said and we know but when you  start seeing it happening it's unnerving  and that's a big increase in the number  of positives out there in the testing  what what are you all trying to do to  sort of help people deal with the  anxiety of those rising numbers well we  we have essentially a wartime president  now and the war is against this  coronavirus we know what we need to do  we need to flatten that curve and we're  confident working together with the  American people doing what the CDC  recommends with government and with  business that we're going to get through  this to the other side of it we are very  strong people I can tell you this more  than we're doing everything possible  working 24/7 either here through  telecommuting and it's gonna be fine we  just got to work to get this through and  everybody's got to pull together there  can't be any dissension in the ranks  the other big thing Martha I got to say  this we got to get that  1.2 trillion dollar stimulus through in  a matter of days not weeks not months  that has to happen now because we know  we're going to have tremendous headwinds  in the second quarter we could get  through that and have a good bounce-back  we need 1.2 trillion dollars the  president is asking for that I think  there's there's basically support for  that on both sides of the aisle they  have to figure out what exactly that  looks like but I'm telling you take days  not weeks yeah you go just the cash that  goes back into people's pockets that we  heard the Treasury secretary talking  about today when they don't have  anywhere to spend it you know how  confident are you all that that's going  to provide a good stimulus if there's no  restaurant there's no movie theater  there's no Broadway show no you know  where does the money go well there's  there's a number of options on the table  one as a payroll tax cut which would be  a sustained a level of support through  the end of the year the other is these  instant rebates and there's a lot of  things in between here's the thing  Martha I've advocated a multi-vector  approach where we have a targeted relief  program like the Pelosi bill was passed  where you help sick people who you don't  want people to have the choice of going  to work sick or staying home broke so  that's that's one vector you got the  fiscal stimulus 1.2 trillion some form  of that you have what the Fed did with  lowering interest rates but also opening  the window for emergency lending they  may need to do that and then you need  the industry assistance and so if you do  all four of those things I got to stress  this more than it's like Congress needs  to understand you can't just do one one  of those things or two of those things  you got to hit all four points of those  companies president is adamant about  this and if we do this within days we're  gonna be just fine economically the most  important thing is the public health  emergency though and that's what I'm  working on the supply chain stuff thank  you for what you're doing Peter Navarro  thank you good to see you tonight

